  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>)</mo></math> True, an atom of each element emits a characteristic spectrum.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>)</mo></math> True, according to Bohr&#39;s postulates&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>r</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>h</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac></math> and hence electron resides into orbits of specific radius called stationary orbits.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>)</mo></math>&nbsp;False, the density of the nucleus is constant.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>)</mo></math> False, A free neutron is unstable decays into proton and electron and antineutrino.</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo></math> True unstable nucleus show radioactivity.</p>",
